you are a young civil engineer standing  on a construction site the Sydney  Harbour Bridge just 200 meters away is  barely visible a dust storm  originating in outback Australia has  blanketed Sydney in thick red dust which  hangs in the air like a San Francisco  fog you love your job from where you  stand from on that construction site  your office is a floating pontoon from  under your hardhat you're responsible  for work crews contract concrete  contractors and men your director of  Australia's largest rural scholarship  foundation you're a dear chairman of the  Sydney civil and structural panel if  these are your headlines they tell a  story of success but on the day that  dust storm blows into Sydney Harbour  your intuition whispers it's a sign as  clear as the visibility of low the  outback has sent you a message and on  this day you listen to your intuition  and you hand in your resignation when  have you listened to your intuition and  made a life-changing decision how will  you know your next job or your next  partner is the one when is your  intuition no longer a whisper but an  idea worthy of a microphone reason and  tradition have led us all to make good  decisions but intuition has led me to  make the greatest decision it just  starts with this little whisper and I'd  love to know what your whispers are and  I'd love to dare you to listen to them  I met J six years earlier in a small  outback town of just 300 people at all  unshaven shaggy head farmer smiled with  his whole body when I walked into that  bar underneath his Akoo bra were hazel  eyes and a big heart  the following week trying to impress a  certain girl a clean-shaven short-haired  farmer smiled with his whole body want a  dance love it was because of listening  to my intuition that I guess I knew what  to do next  standing in that dust on I handed in my  resume resignation I made a decision to  change Einstein said that the intuitive  mind is a sacred gift and the rational  mind is a faithful servant do we live in  a society that honors the servant but  has totally forgotten the gift we are  molded by educational institutions to  make the most rational decisions we are  seduced by a society to believe that  certain career paths will guarantee  success leadership courses preach what  successful careers should look like on a  graph of time versus success we are told  our career trajectories should move in  an upwards direction if I was to leave  Sydney I would drop right off that  career chart could you listen to your  intuition if it was just a whisper  amongst all this noise I resigned and I  followed my heart to outback Australia  to live on a 50,000 acre farm daring to  listen to my intuition help me break up  with the job I loved to chase the man of  my dreams when Harvard Business School  professor clay Christensen looked around  his 20-year reunion  he found an overwhelming proportion of  his classmates were unhappy just as  Einstein had predicted here were those  faithful servants of rational mind  seeking success in the external eyes of  society clay Christensen now teaches MBA  students to listen to themselves when  they make decisions to weigh up success  versus satisfaction walking off that  construction site I learn satisfaction  was the greatest measure for success but  it was the only one that it could  measure it  I arrived on the farm in the middle of  summer temperatures hit 120 degrees my  lipsticks melted across the front seat  of the car as we unpacked our life into  a timber homestead built 180 years ago  only five of the 20 rooms were livable  we were nine hours from Sydney four  hours from the airport two hours from  the nearest traffic light and a mile  from the mailbox a king down there was a  king brown snake under my office desk  and a pet kangaroo in the garden one  afternoon while driving back from  waterskiing the Sun was setting I had my  hand out the window catching the wind  thinking geez that project engineer in  Sydney would still be at work right now  my shoulders relaxed and I was smiling  with my whole body but I had no career  opportunities there were no jobs out  there so I spent a lot of time thinking  about what I really wanted to do and I  started my own engineering company my  engineering passion grabbed my  entrepreneurial spirit by the hand and  led me on a fabulous career adventure  have you had that feeling who would have  thought that that chick that walked off  that Wharf  but headed out to the outback would be  Australia's next young professional  engineer of the year that will create a  three billion dollar rural road plan  Society put me back on that success  chart by following my intuition by  chasing satisfaction I was quickly  accomplishing my dreams and in fact I'd  accomplished all my dreams why would  y'all whisper say at this point picture  the headlines of your future 30 40 and  50 year old self what are your whispers  what are you going to do today to ensure  your milestones milestones go to print  every morning around 10 a.m. Jay would  appear in the office window cut booty  love you can imagine my surprise after  three years of this routine when a  helicopter landed in front of the window  Jay jumped out from behind the controls  want a ride love those three years in  the outback had been the greatest ride  of my life but my intuition was starting  to question the reality of spending the  next 70 years as a farmer's wife versus  expanding my existing business versus  going global with an international  career in engineering what would you  know what to listen to  you are an engineer standing on a farm  in auerbach New South Wales you love  your job you love your lifestyle you  feel pressure to settle but you've made  someone else your first priority and  then you realize you haven't  accomplished all your dreams just the  ones you could reach while patiently  waiting at the bottom of someone else's  list your intuition whispers you're  standing at the river because you want  to cross it if you don't change  direction you will end up where you're  headed that is what I wrote on my  journal the day I left Sydney that is  what I looked at in my journal when I  started my company and perhaps I would  be right to make another decision now  four weeks ago the CEO and founder of  TaskRabbit boomed to the GSB you are the  change you've been waiting for  you're a generation uniquely equipped to  listen to your intuition you are more  equipped than ever and should be more  confident to make these decisions and  stick with them but you are the one you  were the change you've been waiting for  driving out that farm gate  I had no roadmap just this internal  assurance that with every mile I was  rewriting my own headlines I left behind  a house that I'd renovated into a home a  family and a farm that I would never  call mine my two beautiful black  Labradors and my boyfriend of nine and a  half years you intuitive ly know what  you want it's that little whisper that  just says  across the river we're all really scared  I dare you to break up with your  rational mind and chase your real dreams  imagine the adventure you might find  yourself on if you do it today imagine  the day  world leaders first verbalized their  whispers when Barack said to Michelle  want to run for president well when  Hillary said to Bill it's my turn now  babe imagine it is a process of  self-discovery and I love the quote from  Luna gand Australian philosopher come  sit down beside me I said to myself and  although it doesn't make any sense I  held my own hand as a small sign of  trust and together I sat on the fence  but don't sit on the fence too long take  action and just listen today is my 30th  birthday and I'm proud of the headlines  that have gone to print don't wait for  your 20 year reunion to consider what it  means to be satisfied don't wait for  your newspaper to go to print to read  about the headlines that you didn't dare  to chase you don't need a dust storm to  blow through campus you are the change  you've been waiting for  you
 

